 "observations": [
  "The page number «152» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 41», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. The titles (L01-L04), in bold, the sigla in roman; L04, «Locusque1) draconis eius 0 S. 21 G. 54 M.», in smaller bold type, followed by «(CN)». The folios of the manuscripts stand at the left of the last title in two lines, «C 63r, O 120v,» and «N 16v», the letters raised; here as left margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on pp. 150-151 (JP2 0182-0183), with «Pagina numerorum (O) Semita numeri (C)» over the four columns of the arguments, divided below a rule into «Augmentum proprietatum omnium2)» and «Diminutio proprietatum omnium3)» in smaller type; «Sublimatio Martis examinata4)», without «ascensus»; «Examinatio ipsius elheza5)» and «Examinatio centri»; «Statio prima Martis»; «Latitudo Martis accedentis6)» over «prima» and «secunda». Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L05-L09), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L10, the units, in bold.",
  "L11-L40. Checked as on p. 150 (JP2 0182): the Sublimatio is 4s 8° 24′ less half the Examinatio argumenti in every row, a half minute counted as a whole, except at 90°, where 3s 21° 52′ stands for 3s 21° 52½′. The Latitudo secunda is 3° 45′ · sin x to within 0.8″ in every row except at 86°, and 3° 45′ 0″, the greatest latitude of Mars, at 90°. At 86° (L36) the edition prints O's 3° 44′ 26″ (note g), 1.1″ less than 3° 44′ 27.1″; C's 27 agrees with the computation.",
  "L11-L40, the Examinatio argumenti. The radius that reproduces each value as the equation of an epicycle is between 38.99 and 39.04 in every row, against 38.6 to 39.1 on pp. 150-151 (JP2 0182-0183); against the epicycle of radius 38.71 of p. 150 the table is 7.4′ to 13.3′ larger (90°: 33° 3′ for 32° 49.7′).",
  "L11-L40, the other columns, compared from row to row: the Examinatio centri increases by 5′ and 4′ in turn to 73°, then by 3′, 2′ and 1′ a degree, to 11° 13′ at 90°, its greatest value, 3′ more than the 11° 10′ of which 5° 35′ at 30° (p. 150) is the half; it lies between 3.7′ below and 3.0′ above 11° 10′ · sin x. The Statio prima increases from 5s 10° 27′ to 5s 13° 25′ by 5′ to 7′ a degree, mostly 6′. The Latitudo prima decreases from 1° 55′ to 1° 36′ at 90°, by 1′ every second degree to 77° and then by 1′ in most degrees.",
  "Notes on the readings. Note 1: «Locusque», C's reading; N has «Locus autem», as in the title of p. 150 (JP2 0182). Note 6: the head «accedentis» against C's «recedentis» and O's «descendentis». Note a: the last argument, 3s 0°, is N's; C and O have 2. Checked: the Examinatio centri 11° 1′ at 80° (note b, C, against O's 2) and 11° 12′ at 89° (note c, O, against C's 2) agree with the steps of 2′ and 1′ of the column; the degrees 13 of the Statio prima from 87° to 90° (notes d-d, O and N, against C's 12) with the rise of the column; the Latitudo secunda 3° 20′ 29″ at 63° (note e, C, against O's 10) and 3° 33′ 59″ at 72° (note f), 3° 20′ 28.6″ and 3° 33′ 59.3″ computed.",
  "Italics: «om.». N01-N02: the ellipses «. . . .» of the lemmata as printed. The folio «N 16v» without its point (before L05): see the critical note."
 ],
